@startuml 03-ipc-protocol
skinparam backgroundColor White
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Sans
skinparam roundCorner 6
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam ArrowColor #333333
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FFFFF0
skinparam NoteBorderColor #999999
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #F4F4F8
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #555555
skinparam ClassBackgroundColor #F4F4F8
skinparam ClassBorderColor #555555
skinparam StateBackgroundColor #F4F4F8
skinparam StateBorderColor #555555
skinparam ComponentBackgroundColor #F4F4F8
skinparam ComponentBorderColor #555555
title IPC handshake and protocol\nWhat happens between "bytes on a socket" and "a service call"

participant "Client process\n(curl / Python / browser)" as Client
box "moviebackend process" #F8F8F8
    participant "TCP\nConnectionProvider" as Provider
    participant "Server\n(accept loop)" as Server
    participant "HttpConnectionHandler" as Handler
    participant "worker thread\n(one per connection)" as Worker
    participant "HttpRouter" as Router
    participant "ApiController\n(Public / Admin)" as Controller
    participant "RestErrorHandler" as Errors
end box

== Start-up: binding, before any client exists ==
Server -> Provider : construct(host, port)
activate Provider
Provider -> Provider : socket() / bind() / listen()
note right of Provider
  A configured port of 0 asks the OS
  for a free one. oatpp then records
  the real port in a **string property**
  and NOT in the Address struct - which
  is why RestServer::boundPort() reads
  the property.
end note
Provider --> Server : listening
deactivate Provider
Server -> Server : run() - block in accept()

== Connection handshake ==
Client -> Provider : TCP SYN
Provider -> Client : SYN/ACK
Client -> Provider : ACK
Provider --> Server : accepted connection
Server -> Handler : handleConnection(socket)
Handler -> Worker : spawn a thread for this connection
activate Worker

note over Worker #FFE8E8
  **Every controller method can now run
  concurrently with every other one.**
  This is the reason all shared state
  lives behind the Catalog lock.
end note

== Request / response, repeated while the connection is kept alive ==
loop for each request on this connection
    Client -> Worker : GET /api/v1/movies/m1/theaters HTTP/1.1\nHost: ...\nAccept: application/json

    Worker -> Worker : parse the request line and headers
    Worker -> Router : resolve("GET", "/api/v1/movies/{movieId}/theaters")

    alt route matches
        Router --> Worker : endpoint + path parameters {movieId:"m1"}
        Worker -> Controller : invoke the ENDPOINT method

        activate Controller
        alt body expected
            Worker -> Worker : deserialise JSON body into a DTO
        end

        alt the call succeeds
            Controller -> Controller : map DTO -> domain, call the service,\nmap the result -> DTO
            Controller --> Worker : 2xx + DTO
            Worker -> Worker : serialise the DTO to JSON
            Worker -> Client : HTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{...}
        else a ServiceError is thrown
            Controller -->x Worker : ServiceError propagates
            note right of Controller
              Controllers contain **no try/catch**.
              That is deliberate: one place owns the
              error-to-status mapping.
            end note
            Worker -> Errors : handleError(exception_ptr)
            activate Errors
            Errors -> Errors : rethrow, catch, map ErrorCode -> Status\n(NotFound->404, InvalidArgument->400,\nConflict/SeatUnavailable->409, ...)
            Errors --> Worker : response + ErrorDto
            deactivate Errors
            Worker -> Client : HTTP/1.1 409 Conflict\n\n{"code":"SEAT_UNAVAILABLE",\n "message":"seat 'a1' is already booked"}
        end
        deactivate Controller

    else no route matches
        Router --> Worker : no mapping
        Worker -> Errors : handleError(404, "No mapping for ...")
        Errors --> Worker : response + ErrorDto
        Worker -> Client : HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found\n\n{"code":"NOT_FOUND", ...}
    end
end

== Teardown ==
Client -> Worker : connection closed (or Connection: close)
Worker -> Worker : thread exits
deactivate Worker

== Shutdown, on SIGINT / SIGTERM ==
[-> Server : signal handler sets an atomic flag;\na watcher thread calls RestServer::requestStop()
note right of Server
  The handler does exactly one legal thing:
  a lock-free atomic store.

  It must not call requestStop() directly -
  that logs, the logger takes a mutex, and a
  handler can interrupt a thread already
  holding it. The watcher thread runs the
  real shutdown, where locking is safe.
end note
Server -> Provider : stop()  (closes the listening socket)
Provider --> Server : accept() returns
Server --> Server : run() returns
Server -> Handler : stop()  (waits for in-flight requests)
Handler -> Worker : join every connection thread

@enduml
